## CSS & Styling Guidelines

- Follow the **BEM (Block Element Modifier)** naming convention for all CSS class names.  
  **Example:** `ft-button`, `ft-button__icon`, `ft-button--primary`

- All custom classes must start with the prefix **`ft-`**.  
  **Example:** ✅ `ft-card__header`, ❌ `card-header`

- Before creating a new class, **always check** if an existing one already covers the same use case.  
  Reuse and extend existing classes when possible.

- Prefer **component-level styles (scoped CSS)** instead of global styles.

- Use **CSS variables** (`--ft-color-primary`, `--ft-spacing-md`, etc.) for colors, spacing, and typography to ensure design consistency.

- **Do not hardcode** colors, font sizes, or spacing directly in CSS. Instead, use the shared variables defined in **`variables.scss`**, which include:
  - Color variables  
  - Text and typography variables  
  - Spacing and layout variables  
  These must be used when creating or customizing styles.

- **Always prioritize using existing theme utilities** before creating custom styles.  
  Specifically, use:
  - **Layout classes** (e.g., display, flex, gap, margin, padding)  
  - **Typography classes** (e.g., font size, color, alignment, text transform)  
  - **Theme-related classes** (e.g., text color utilities, background colors)  
  These ensure consistency across components and reduce redundant CSS.

- Avoid inline styles and `ngStyle`. Use **style bindings** or **class bindings** instead.

- Use **utility classes** only for layout adjustments — not for component-specific styling.

- Keep CSS declarations **minimal and purposeful** — avoid deep nesting and excessive specificity.

- When defining responsive layouts, prefer **Grid or Flex** and use defined CSS variables for breakpoints.

- Use **transitions and animations sparingly**; prioritize performance and accessibility.

- For **icons and images**:
  - Prefer Angular’s `NgOptimizedImage`
  - Always include `alt` attributes for accessibility
